" Editor defaults for writing Kubernetes YAML under time pressure.
"
" Debian sources this file last, after /etc/vim/vimrc and after
" defaults.vim, so everything here wins. It is shipped rather than left to
" defaults for two reasons.
"
" First, determinism: Debian's syntax highlighting arrives via
" defaults.vim, which vim silently SKIPS the moment any ~/.vimrc exists. A
" candidate who writes one line of personal config would lose colour on
" every manifest and never connect the two. Setting it here does not care
" whether a ~/.vimrc turns up.
"
" Second, tabs. A literal tab is invalid YAML, and the failure is
" miserable to read: the parser rejects the document with a line number
" and no explanation a candidate under a clock will enjoy. The filetype
" plugin already gets this right for *.yaml, but not for a manifest opened
" as a new unnamed buffer and written later, which is exactly how people
" work in an exam.

syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" Two spaces, never a tab. Applied globally, not just to *.yaml, so a
" buffer that has not been named yet still cannot produce one.
set expandtab
set tabstop=2
set shiftwidth=2
set softtabstop=2
set autoindent

" Nothing that changes what the candidate sees relative to a real exam
" beyond legibility: no plugins, no colorscheme override, no keymaps.
set number
set ruler
set laststatus=2

" Trailing whitespace and tabs made visible in YAML, where both are
" invisible causes of a rejected manifest.
"
" ASCII on purpose. This file is sourced before 'encoding' is settled, so
" a multibyte glyph here is read as its raw bytes and vim rejects the
" option with E1511 ("wrong number of characters for field") — which then
" prints on every single vim launch.
autocmd FileType yaml setlocal list listchars=tab:>-,trail:.
